 based on evidence.\n\nLet's evaluate: B has proven stress handling and conscientiousness, but interpersonal issues. C has reliability and decision quality, but communication issues and lack of stress evidence. D has responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but arrogance and weak leadership, and unknown stress resilience. A has technical strengths but no stress data and organizational issues.\n\nWhich candidate best fits overall? The key criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nB: reliability (conscientious), stress resilience (handles stress), technical competence (weather assessment, computer skills), decision quality unknown, attention unknown, crew cooperation (nasty remarks, uncooperative), communication (tone issues), responsibility unknown, adaptability unknown.\n\nC: reliability (100% reliable), stress resilience unknown, technical competence unknown, decision quality (quick correct decisions), attention unknown, crew cooperation (positive atmosphere), communication difficulty, egocentric, adaptability low (not willing to further education).\n\nD: reliability unknown, stress resilience (responds adequately to unexpected events), technical competence (problem solving), decision quality unknown, attention (concentrates well), crew cooperation (arrogant, weak leadership), communication unknown, responsibility (takes seriously), adaptability unknown.\n\nA: reliability unknown, stress resilience unknown, technical competence (anticipates danger, complex connections, spatial vision), decision quality unknown, attention (unorganized), crew cooperation (leadership but show-off, not open to new ideas), communication unknown, responsibility unknown, adaptability low.\n\nGiven the lack of stress evidence for C and D, B seems strongest on stress. B's interpersonal issues are serious but perhaps mitigable. C's communication and egocentric may be more severe for crew safety. D's arrogance and weak leadership also problematic.\n\nThus B likely remains best. As Anna, I might shift my vote to B, aligning with panel majority. I should reference that we have no stress data for C, and B's stress handling is proven, and that B's interpersonal issues can be mitigated. Also note D's arrogance and weak leadership are concerns.\n\nThus I will change my vote to Candidate B, and provide reasoning.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: B handles stress very well; B very conscientious; B makes nasty remarks; C 100% reliable; C makes correct decisions quickly; C difficulty communicating; C egocentric; C not willing to further education; D responds adequately to unexpected events; D takes responsibility seriously; D regarded as arrogant; D weak leadership; A anticipates dangerous situations; A unorganized; A not open to new ideas.\n\nWe need to reference prior discussion: Agent 2's point about lack of stress data for A, and Agent 3's support for B.\n\nThus produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: \"I now align with B as leading candidate because...\" and METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate B.\n\nProceed."}
